  "id": "Compound::DB01098 - Disease::DOID:1936",
  "verbalization": "Rosuvastatin is implicated in the pathogenesis of atherosclerosis through a shared mechanism involving Ezetimibe, an antilipidemic agent. The evidence suggests that Rosuvastatin causes cognitive impairment, immune-mediated necrotising myopathy, and forgetfulness, all of which are mediated by Ezetimibe. Ezetimibe, in turn, treats atherosclerosis, indicating a potential link between the two entities through a common pathway. The lowest common ancestor of Rosuvastatin and Ezetimibe is the antilipidemic agent and an organonitrogen heterocyclic compound, reinforcing the connection between these entities. The consistent role of Ezetimibe as a mediator across different effects of Rosuvastatin supports the hypothesis that it may influence atherosclerosis through a broader biological mechanism, possibly involving lipid metabolism or immune modulation. The evidence suggests a potential causal relationship, but the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
